# Project Athena .trivyignore — ATHENA-126
#
# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 (msgpack: "Out-of-bounds read / crash on Unpacker
# reuse after a caught error", fixed upstream at msgpack==1.2.1):
#
# NOT an application dependency. No requirements.in/.txt/pyproject.toml in
# this repo pins `msgpack` (`grep -rn '^msgpack' -- requirements*.txt`
# across the whole tree returns nothing; the one `msgpack`-adjacent hit,
# `ormsgpack==1.12.2` in src/orchestrator/requirements.txt, is an unrelated
# Rust-backed package with its own advisory history). Trivy finds it
# because pip itself vendors msgpack==1.1.2 internally
# (pip/_vendor/msgpack/, used only by pip's own vendored CacheControl for
# HTTP wheel-cache bookkeeping during `pip install`) and ships a CycloneDX
# SBOM (pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json) that Trivy's Python scanner reads.
#
# Confirmed against the latest released pip (26.2.1, pinned in every
# Dockerfile in this repo): its vendor.txt still lists msgpack==1.1.2.
# pip's own `main` branch has already bumped the vendored copy to 1.2.1,
# but that fix has not shipped in any released pip version as of
# 2026-09-28 (`pip index versions pip` — latest is 26.2.1) — there is no
# pip version we can pin today that clears this.
#
# No application code in this repo imports pip's vendor tree (`grep -rn
# "pip\._vendor"` across src/admin/apps returns nothing), so the vulnerable
# code path is reachable only from a `pip install` invocation at build
# time, never from the running service.
#
# Re-check: `pip index versions pip`. Remove this entry once a released
# pip vendors msgpack>=1.2.1, then bump the `pip==` pin in every Dockerfile
# (and scripts/generate-rag-dockerfiles.py's template) to that release.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools: path traversal in PackageIndex, fixed
# upstream at setuptools==78.1.1):
#
# Same shape as GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 above, confirmed with
# `trivy image --list-all-pkgs`: this image reports TWO setuptools
# entries — `setuptools==84.0.0` at
# usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/setuptools-84.0.0.dist-info
# (this repo's explicit `pip install ... setuptools==84.0.0` pin, already
# well above the fix floor, real FilePath on disk) and a second
# `setuptools==70.3.0` with FilePath `None` — that second one is pip's own
# internally-vendored copy (pip/_vendor/setuptools/, per pip 26.2.1's
# vendor.txt), reported only via pip's CycloneDX SBOM
# (pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json), not a real installed distribution. Our
# top-level setuptools pin cannot touch pip's internal vendor copy — only
# a pip release that re-vendors setuptools>=78.1.1 can, and pip 26.2.1
# (latest released, pinned in every Dockerfile here) still vendors 70.3.0.
#
# CVE-2025-47273 is a path-traversal bug in setuptools' legacy
# `PackageIndex` / easy_install network-fetch code
# (setuptools/package_index.py) — pip's own installer does not call that
# code path (pip has used its own resolver/HTTP stack since pip 20.3, not
# setuptools' PackageIndex), so the vendored copy is dead weight pip
# carries for backward compatibility, not a code path this repo's builds
# or runtime ever execute.
#
# Re-check: `pip index versions pip`. Remove this entry once a released
# pip vendors setuptools>=78.1.1.
CVE-2025-47273
